  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Monday)\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nDry and fairly pleasant conditions are expected for Tuesday night \nthrough Wednesday night as the upper-level flow becomes quasi-zonal \nwith persistent southerly surface flow.  High temperatures will be \nin the low 80s with lows in the low 60s.  However, conditions are \nexpected to change substantially for Thursday into Friday. The \nupper-level flow will become southwesterly again as models show a\nbroad trough developing over northwest Mexico and the Baja. \nMeanwhile, Tropical Storm Vance, currently located in the eastern \nPacific, will be sheared apart before reaching the west coast of \nMexico but the energy and moisture will be carried into west Texas\nby Thursday afternoon. Multiple rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms are expected to develop Thursday through Thursday \nnight. Chances for severe weather will be low but model consensus\nshows PWAT values of 1.2-1.6 inches so heavy rainfall will be the\nmain concern. Ensembles show 24-hour rainfall amounts ranging \nfrom a half inch to 1.5 inches from midday Thursday to midday \nFriday but these amounts could change over the next 48 hours. This\ncould lead to areas of flooding, especially for the Concho Valley\nand Edwards Plateau. Rain chances will continue into Friday \nmorning but should decrease through Saturday afternoon. Overall \nlow (<20%) chances for rain will continue into Sunday with \notherwise near-normal temperatures.",
